{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ columbia university 1968 occupation ida gym)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_3164994015#5_3495006769","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3787","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_3164994015#5_3495006769\" , \"end_char\": 7348.000000 , \"headings\": \"Columbia University protests of 1968<cover>Columbia University protests of 1968 Contents Background Discovery of IDA documents Morningside Park gymnasium Protests Occupation of Hamilton Hall Activist separation Popular responses Suppression of protesters Second round of protests Aftermath Immediate responses Long term effects In popular culture See also References Further reading External links \" , \"segment\": \"It was for these reasons that the project was labelled by some as \\\"Gym </cover>Crow\". Since 1958 the University had evicted more than seven thousand Harlem residents from Columbia-controlled properties—85 percent of whom were African American or Puerto Rican. Many Harlem residents paid rent to the University. Black students at a 40th anniversary event said their bitterness evolved from discrimination, that unlike white students their identifications were constantly checked, and that black women were told not to register for difficult courses. A \"stacking system\" that put all the former black football players in the same position was described. Protests Play media Part one of Columbia Revolt Play media Part "}}
